[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Structs, Methods, and Interfaces in Go"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Go approaches object-oriented programming differently than traditional languages like Java or C++. Instead of class hierarchies, Go relies on composition via structs, receiver functions for methods, and implicit satisfaction through interfaces. This tutorial explores how these three pillars work together."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Defining and Using Structs"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A struct is a typed collection of fields. They are useful for grouping data together to form records. Unlike classes, structs do not have built-in inheritance, favoring composition instead."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"package main

import "fmt"

type User struct {
    Name  string
    Email string
    Age   int
}

func main() {
    u := User{
        Name:  "Alice",
        Email: "alice@example.com",
        Age:   30,
    }
    fmt.Printf("User: %+v\n", u)
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Adding Behavior with Methods"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"In Go, a method is a function with a special receiver argument. The receiver appears in its own argument list between the func keyword and the method name. Receivers can be value types or pointer types."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"package main

import "fmt"

type Rectangle struct {
    Width, Height float64
}

// Value receiver (reads data)
func (r Rectangle) Area() float64 {
    return r.Width * r.Height
}

// Pointer receiver (modifies data)
func (r *Rectangle) Scale(factor float64) {
    r.Width *= factor
    r.Height *= factor
}

func main() {
    rect := Rectangle{Width: 10, Height: 5}
    fmt.Println("Area:", rect.Area())
    rect.Scale(2)
    fmt.Println("New Area:", rect.Area())
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Implementing Interfaces"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"An interface is a set of method signatures. When a type provides definitions for all the methods in an interface, it is said to implement that interface. Crucially, Go interfaces are satisfied implicitly--no "implements" keyword is required."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"package main

import "fmt"

type Greeter interface {
    Greet() string
}

type Person struct {
    Name string
}

func (p Person) Greet() string {
    return "Hello, my name is " + p.Name
}

func PrintGreeting(g Greeter) {
    fmt.Println(g.Greet())
}

func main() {
    p := Person{Name: "Bob"}
    PrintGreeting(p)
}"},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Structs aggregate state into cohesive units without class-based inheritance."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Methods attach behavior to structs using value or pointer receivers."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Interfaces decouple components by defining behavior contracts that types satisfy implicitly."}]
